= Attention — Technical =

== Selection dimensions ==
Attention can select spatial location, object, feature, modality, task rule, time, or internal representation.

These dimensions recruit overlapping but non-identical mechanisms.

== Endogenous and exogenous control ==
Endogenous control reflects current goals and expectations.

Exogenous capture reflects stimulus-driven priority.

Modern models often describe a priority map integrating bottom-up salience, top-down goals, learned value, and selection history.

== Capacity and bottlenecks ==
Interference can arise at perceptual, central, memory, or response-selection stages.

Dual-task costs therefore do not imply one global fixed attentional resource.

== Artificial-attention contrast ==
Transformer attention is a differentiable weighting mechanism over representations.

It was named by analogy but is not equivalent to human cognitive attention.

